      {
        "id": 80527,
        "name": "Foote",
        "username": "Foote",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/foote/{size}/1877_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-11-04T19:53:45.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>So I have been thinking about this non-stop at work (I know, I am compulsive. Sorry).</p><p>Consider the following sentence: \"Hey Boss, I will be out of the office until Thursday\"</p><p>It sure sounds like I will be in the office Thursday, does it not? If you told your boss that and was not there on Thursday, I would think you would be getting fired.</p><p>The more I think about it, the more I dislike the interpretation that \"until\" means \"up to and including\". I know I have flip flopped on this topic a few times in this thread, but theres a lot of nuance to it. I think given the rest of the context, it should not be interpreted as \"up to and including\". So that probably now puts me into the camp of Mistbound ends right before the Start of your Turn. Ugh.</p>",
        "post_number": 28,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:02:41.484Z",
