      {
        "id": 80531,
        "name": "Foote",
        "username": "Foote",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/foote/{size}/1877_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-11-05T02:37:04.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Spiff\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/spiff/48/1865_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Spiff:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<blockquote>\nI think the correct interpretation is that 'until the start ...' means that the effect ends just before the Start of turn phase.\n</blockquote>\nIf someone said \"I'll wait for you until 2pm\", they don't mean they're going to leave at 1:59pm, they mean that they will wait until 2pm clicks over and then they will leave.&nbsp; I think that if you're doing something \"until the start of your turn\", the start of your turn (the trigger to end the until-loop) has to actually occur before the \"until-ed\" thing will end.\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Would you agree or disagree that at 2 you are no longer waiting but at 159 you are? That seems to me what you are saying. If 2 is the start of turn and your waiting status is the mistbound effect in this analogy, then maybe there is another way to view it.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>English is a terrible language this way isn't it.&nbsp;</p>",
